 ## Background
-Bounding Volume Optimization originated in the context of raymarching and voxel rendering to address the high cost of evaluating Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) for every primitive in a scene. It was notably discussed in works on raycasting-based voxel rendering and CUDA-based SDF raymarching [S1, unofficial][S2]. The technique is particularly relevant for real-time applications where frame rates are critical, such as in game development, where it allows for the separation of game world and entities, enabling physics simulations using voxel models as rigid bodies [S1, unofficial].
+Bounding Volume Optimization was introduced to minimize the cost of the map function by skipping SDF evaluations for non-contributing objects [S2], and to adapt voxel array dimensions to actual data extents [S1, unofficial]. It was notably discussed in works on raycasting-based voxel rendering and CUDA-based SDF raymarching [S1, unofficial][S2]. The technique is particularly relevant for real-time applications where frame rates are critical, such as in game development, where it allows for the separation of game world and entities, enabling physics simulations using voxel models as rigid bodies [S1, unofficial].
 
